HST Office: the coat draws tree and list views, sliders, progress bars, group frames, popup menus' items and tooltips in the HST look.

- Only the drawing changes. Every size, item height, indent, border width and content region stays the
  platform's and VCL's, so no dialog's layout changes. The coat's switch (org.openoffice.Office.HSTAppearance/
  UiCoat/Enabled, HST_UI_COAT=0/1) turns it off, and high contrast always keeps the platform's look.
- Through OutputDevice::DrawNativeControl (where the platform draws these natively):
  - a tree node's expander: a chevron in the muted text colour (the selected text's on a selected row);
  - a slider: a thin groove, the accent up to the thumb, a round thumb (a ring around an accent dot) in the
    thumb rectangle VCL computed, accent when hovered, two pixels when focused;
  - a progress bar (dialogs and the status bar): a rounded track, the accent up to the value;
  - a popup menu drawn by VCL (Windows): a flat menu surface, the highlighted item inset and rounded, a thin
    separator, vector check and radio marks for checked items, a chevron for a submenu. The menus' outline,
    rounded edge and shadow are left to the frame's menus part (coat-menus.cxx documents where).
- OutputDevice::IsNativeControlSupported (thin hook, hst::coat::ClaimsNativeControl): while the coat draws, it
  claims a tooltip's background and a submenu arrow beside natively drawn menu items, so VCL asks it for them;
  VCL measures both itself when the platform gives no region, so no size changes.
- Thin hooks where VCL draws without asking the platform:
  - SvTreeListBox::PaintEntry1 and ImplListBoxWindow::ImplPaint: a selected row is a rounded shape (a row
    across columns rounded at its ends only). The theme's highlight colour gives way to the HST selection
    colour (light: the accent; dark: the selected-item tint) while the selected text stays readable (4.5:1);
    an unfocused list's colour is kept;
  - VclScrolledWindow::Paint: the frame around a list, a tree or a preview is one control-border line with a
    field's soft corners, in VCL's border width;
  - DecorationView::DrawSeparator: a separator control (FixedLine: a group's heading line) draws one thin line
    instead of the two-colour 3D line.
- GTK 3 (welded dialogs): the same tokens as CSS for tree views and lists (hover tint, selection), scales, progress bars, frames and
  separators, menu items (highlight, marks, arrow, shortcut) and tooltips: colours and corner radii only.
- The logic is in vcl/source/hst/coat/{coat-lists,coat-ranges,coat-menus,coat-groups-tooltips,
  coat-gtk-css-lists-menus}.cxx; shared shapes (check glyph, mark box, contrast ratio, selection colours) in
  coat-shapes.cxx.
- CppunitTest_vcl_hst_coat_controls2: the expander's direction and colour, the selection's shape and colour
  choice, the slider's groove, value and thumb states, the progress, the menu's surface, highlight, separator,
  marks and arrow, the tooltip, the claims (only while drawing), the scrolled window's border, the separator
  control only, everything off in high contrast and when switched off, and the GTK CSS.
- GTK 3 buttons: a theme's primary action (suggested-action, e.g. the print panel's Print button) is the accent
  button with its ink; the coat's light button fill had covered the theme's coloured fill and left its light text
  unreadable.
- VCL tree and list views (SvTreeListBox: Navigator, Styles, Options tree, the File tab's lists on Windows): the row
  under the mouse gets a pale rounded tint. The row is looked up on each mouse move; only when it changes are the
  row it left and the row it entered invalidated, inside the rows' area (the scroll bars do not repaint with them).
  Tracked only while the coat draws and not when the list selects by hovering. CppunitTest_vcl_hst_coat_controls2
  also covers the hover (following, leaving, hover selection, switched off, the painted tint, the invalidated row)
  and prints its repaint cost per mouse move and per one-row scroll, coat on/off.
- The rounded selection and hover of a row are anti-aliased only in the columns their rounded ends reach; the
  straight middle is a plain rectangle. The pixels are those of the whole rounded shape (the test compares every
  pixel for many row sizes and ends), at a fraction of the cost, which keeps the hover within the repaint budget.

